          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+geometry"} analytic geometry
            is a developing subject that gives an alternative/complementary approach to scheme theory in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+number+theory"} analytic number theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%A9r%C3%B4me+Poineau"} Jérôme Poineau
          %p Many interesting results on polynomial equations can be proven using the mysterious interactions between algebraic, complex analytic and p-adic analytic geometry. The aim of global analytic geometry is to construct a category of spaces which contains these three geometries.
          %h2#Motivation Motivation
          %p Global analytic geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                combines
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-archimedean+analytic+geometry"} non-archimedean
                and archimedean
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+geometry"} analytic geometry
            %li
              %p
                contains
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
                as a sub-theory;
            %li
              %p
                treats all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/places"} places
                on equal footing, contrary to scheme theory. This implies that it is closer in spirit to the automorphic philosophy and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+program"} Langlands program
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+equation"} functional equation
          %p
            One aim of the theory is to define, using global analytic tools, a good
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            for arithmetic varieties.
          %h2#possible_set_of_initial_constraints Possible set of initial constraints
          %p
            For a
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relaxation_%28approximation%29"} relaxed
            approach to global Hodge theory: it is not an easy task to find a good set of constraints on such a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+Hodge+theory"} global Hodge theory
          %ol
            %li
              having a good theory of linear coefficients on global analytic spaces, with the Grothendieck six operations (this should be done by the use of the étale
              %span.newWikiWord
                sub-analytic
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/sub-analytic"} ?
              topology in characteristic
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              %span.newWikiWord
                definable
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/definable"} ?
              sets for the étale G-topology on strict and non-strict overconvergent analytic spaces). It seems that global analytic motivic spectral coefficients (given by imposing homotopy invariance with respect to the unit disc are not so well adapted to the study of torsion phenomena in the characteristic p situation).
            %li
              having a good theory of higher and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+global+analytic+geometry"} derived global analytic geometry
          %ul
            %li get back (or be isomorphic to) the usual algebraic de Rham Chern character when one works with usual schemes.
            %li get back the p-adic analytic de Rham Chern character (on Ayoub’s motivic cohomology) of dagger spaces when one works with dagger p-adic spaces.
            %li
              get back the usual de Rham Chern character when one works over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \C
          %p
            This first set of constraints is worked out in the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overconvergent+global+analytic+geometry"} overconvergent global analytic geometry
          %p
            The problems related to the torsion phenomena for coefficients in characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            may be perhaps be solved by the development of a really non-linear analytic homotopy theory, meaning a proper theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+global+analytic+geometry"} spectral global analytic geometry
          %h2#a_more_optimistic_set_of_constraints A more optimistic set of constraints
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                having a theory of derived analytic microlocalization of sheaves and differential equations, allowing the proper settlement of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+index+theory"} global analytic index theory
            %li
              %p
                being able to prove the functional equation of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+functions"} zeta functions
                of arbitrary
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+varieties"} arithmetic varieties
            %li
              %p
                being able to settle down an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+langlands+program"} analytic langlands program
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+representations"} automorphic representations
                and a sort of global
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+motives"} analytic motives
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_p
            %li
              %p
                being able to devise a robust and simple enough
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+cryptography"} arithmetic cryptography
                protocol based on a
                %span.newWikiWord
                  discrete logarithm problem
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/discrete+logarithm+problem"} ?
                or on a
                %span.newWikiWord
                  cohomological product problem
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cohomological+product+problem"} ?
                on a given
                %span.newWikiWord
                  geometric cohomology
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometric+cohomology"} ?
                theory for global analytic spaces.
          %p Argument in favor of its use are:
          %ul
            %li the fact that archimedean factors are deeply related to (real and complex analytic)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"}> Hodge theory
            ;
          %ul
            %li
              the fact that all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
              of parts of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Langlands+program"} local Langlands program
              use deeply non-archimedean analytic spaces that are out of the scope of classical algebraic scheme theory.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berkovich+space"}> Berkovich space
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Huber+space"} Huber space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfectoid+space"} perfectoid space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arakelov+geometry"} Arakelov geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+field+analogy"} function field analogy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arithmetic+cryptography"} Arithmetic cryptography
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+index+theory"} global analytic index theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overconvergent+global+analytic+geometry"} overconvergent global analytic geometry
            %li
              %p
